issue.body:
as @massimilianomancini mentioned [in this comment](https://github.com/yiisoft/yii2/issues/6553#issuecomment-71180691) there are some cases when the validation with ICU format is not correct.

For example:

``` php
$validator = new DateValidator;
$validator->format = 'yyyy-MM-dd';
var_dump($validator->validate('14-12-11')); // boolean true

$validator = new DateValidator;
$validator->format = 'yy-MM-dd';
var_dump($validator->validate('2014-12-11')); // boolean true

$validator = new DateValidator;
$validator->format = 'yy-MM-dd';
var_dump($validator->validate('214-12-11')); // boolean true, timestamp: -55384304008, year: 0214

$validator = new DateValidator;
$validator->format = 'yMM-dd';
var_dump($validator->validate('2014-12-11')); // boolean true ?!

$validator = new DateValidator;
$validator->format = 'php:Y-m-d';
var_dump($validator->validate('14-12-11')); // boolean true, short year accepted?

$validator = new DateValidator;
$validator->format = 'php:y-m-d';
var_dump($validator->validate('2014-12-11')); // boolean false, correct
```

 What I'm trying to say, is that a strict validation of mysql date format is nearly impossible at the moment. Probably in most of those cases `$timestampAttribute` would have a wrong year value, for example 14 as year could be interpreted as 2014 instead of 1914.
